    id: 2,
    title: "NSSF Contributions in Kenya (2026): Rates, Limits and Examples",
    slug: "the-complete-guide-to-nssf-contributions-in-kenya",
    metaTitle: "NSSF Kenya 2026: Contributions, Tiers and Limits",
    metaDescription: "Updated NSSF Kenya 2026 rates: 6% per employee and employer, KSh 9,000 lower and KSh 108,000 upper earnings limits, with worked contribution examples.",
    content: "<p><strong>Reviewed 22 October 2026.</strong> Kenya's National Social Security Fund (NSSF) collects pension contributions from eligible employees and their employers. The contribution percentage is 6% per side; the income limits used to calculate the contribution increased on 1 February 2026 under the fourth implementation phase of the NSSF Act.</p>\n<h2>NSSF contribution rates effective February 2026</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Monthly amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lower earnings limit (LEL)</td><td>KSh 9,000</td></tr><tr><td>Upper earnings limit (UEL)</td><td>KSh 108,000</td></tr><tr><td>Employee Tier I maximum</td><td>KSh 540</td></tr><tr><td>Employee Tier II maximum</td><td>KSh 5,940</td></tr><tr><td>Employee total maximum</td><td>KSh 6,480</td></tr><tr><td>Employer total maximum</td><td>KSh 6,480</td></tr><tr><td>Employee and employer combined maximum</td><td>KSh 12,960</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The official NSSF notice specifies these figures for Year Four, beginning February 2026. The contribution percentage itself did not increase; the upper and lower pensionable-earnings limits did. Source: <a href=\"https://www.mygov.go.ke/sites/default/files/2026-02/MyGov%20February%2017%2C%202026.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">NSSF notice to employers, February 2026</a>.</p>\n<h2>How to calculate your monthly contribution</h2>\n<p>For employees earning up to KSh 108,000 in pensionable pay, the employee contribution is generally 6% of the relevant pensionable earnings. The maximum employee contribution is KSh 6,480 per month. The employer pays an equal contribution separately; the employer's share is not an additional deduction from the employee's net salary.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Monthly pensionable earnings</th><th>Employee pays</th><th>Employer pays</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>KSh 15,000</td><td>KSh 900</td><td>KSh 900</td></tr><tr><td>KSh 50,000</td><td>KSh 3,000</td><td>KSh 3,000</td></tr><tr><td>KSh 100,000</td><td>KSh 6,000</td><td>KSh 6,000</td></tr><tr><td>KSh 150,000</td><td>KSh 6,480 (capped)</td><td>KSh 6,480 (capped)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The examples assume the gross amount shown is pensionable earnings and do not account for scheme-specific arrangements or exemptions. If you have contracted-out arrangements or unusual employment terms, check your payroll officer and the NSSF directly.</p>\n<h2>What changed from 2025?</h2>\n<p>Between February 2025 and January 2026, the monthly upper earnings limit was KSh 72,000 and the maximum employee contribution was KSh 4,320. From February 2026, these became KSh 108,000 and KSh 6,480 respectively. An employee whose pensionable pay exceeds the upper limit therefore contributes up to KSh 2,160 more each month than at the previous maximum. Lower earners who were already paying 6% of their full pensionable pay may not see a change.</p>\n<h2>When must employers remit NSSF?</h2>\n<p>NSSF's notice requires the employer to remit contributions by the ninth day of the following month. Employers should reconcile the employee and matching employer contribution, retain payment records and verify any changes in scheme or payroll treatment using <a href=\"https://www.nssf.or.ke/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">NSSF's official website</a>.</p>\n<h2>How does NSSF affect take-home pay?</h2>\n<p>NSSF is only one component of payroll. PAYE, SHIF and the Affordable Housing Levy have separate rules. Do not estimate your net salary by subtracting NSSF alone, and do not confuse the combined employee-and-employer maximum of KSh 12,960 with the employee's own maximum of KSh 6,480. For other payroll issues, see our <a href=\"/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paye-tax-in-kenya/\">PAYE guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Is NSSF 12% of my salary?</h3><p>The scheme combines 6% employee and 6% employer contributions. Only the employee's own share is deducted from their pay under ordinary payroll arrangements.</p>\n<h3>What is the maximum employee deduction in 2026?</h3><p>KSh 6,480 per month from February 2026 for employees whose pensionable earnings reach or exceed KSh 108,000.</p>\n<h3>Did the NSSF rate rise in February 2026?</h3><p>The contribution percentage remained 6% per side. Pensionable earnings thresholds increased in the next implementation phase.</p>\n<p><em>Last reviewed: 22 October 2026. Rules and administrative instructions may change, so verify any financial decisions with NSSF.</em></p>",
