    content: "<h2>Take-home pay on a KSh 200,000.00 monthly salary</h2><p><strong>Updated 22 October 2026.</strong> Kenya's monthly PAYE bands published by KRA are 10% on the first KSh 24,000 of taxable pay, 25% on the next KSh 8,333, 30% on the next KSh 467,667, 32.5% on the next KSh 300,000 and 35% on the balance above KSh 800,000. Resident personal relief is generally KSh 2,400 per month. The bands apply progressively, not as one flat rate.</p><p>Payroll deductions such as NSSF, SHIF and the Affordable Housing Levy should be treated according to the applicable tax rules, rather than being combined into a flat percentage. <a href=\"https://www.kra.go.ke/individual/filing-paying/types-of-taxes/paye\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">Source: KRA PAYE</a>.</p><h2>Worked payslip example</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Monthly amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gross salary</td><td>KSh 200,000.00</td></tr><tr><td>Employee NSSF (6%, capped)</td><td>−KSh 6,480.00</td></tr><tr><td>SHIF (2.75%)</td><td>−KSh 5,500.00</td></tr><tr><td>Affordable Housing Levy (1.5%)</td><td>−KSh 3,000.00</td></tr><tr><td>Illustrative taxable earnings</td><td>KSh 185,020.00</td></tr><tr><td>PAYE after monthly personal relief</td><td>−KSh 47,889.35</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Illustrative net salary</strong></td><td><strong>KSh 137,130.65</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How the estimate is calculated</h2><p>The employee NSSF contribution is generally 6% of pensionable pay, capped at KSh 6,480 per month in the February 2026 phase. SHIF is calculated at 2.75% of gross for salaried employees, and the employee Affordable Housing Levy is 1.5%. Under KRA's published rules, qualifying SHIF and AHL payments and eligible registered pension contributions reduce taxable employment income before PAYE is computed. The employer's own statutory contributions are not deducted from the employee's gross pay.</p><p>These illustrations assume an ordinary resident employee, one month of constant gross and pensionable earnings, no bonus or non-cash benefits, no extra voluntary pension, mortgage or insurance relief, and the 2026 NSSF earnings limits. Your own payroll may legitimately differ. See <a href=\"/blog/the-complete-guide-to-nssf-contributions-in-kenya/\">NSSF rates</a> and <a href=\"/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paye-tax-in-kenya/\">the PAYE guide</a>.</p><h2>What might change the actual amount?</h2><p>Taxable allowances, insurance relief, mortgage interest, post-retirement medical contributions, additional qualifying pension contributions, arrears, loan repayments and other pay deductions can change your net salary. Check your individual PAYE and NSSF line items before relying on any example.</p><p><em>Reviewed 22 October 2026. KRA is the authority for tax treatment; your payslip and employer's payroll records determine your specific deductions.</em></p>",
