    metaDescription: "Kenya Significant Economic Presence Tax: how SEPT replaced DST in December 2024, who it affects, the 3% system calculation and compliance cautions.",
    content: "<h2>Kenya's Significant Economic Presence Tax (SEPT)</h2><p>Kenya introduced Significant Economic Presence Tax, commonly abbreviated SEPT or SEP tax, in place of the Digital Service Tax regime under the Tax Laws (Amendment) Act 2024. KRA's iTax documentation states that the SEP obligation took effect from <strong>27 December 2024</strong>, while DST remains relevant to earlier periods.</p><h2>Who may be affected?</h2><p>SEP focuses on specified income earned by non-resident persons through digital or electronic-network services accessed or consumed in Kenya. The exact scope, exclusions, taxpayer status and applicable rules need to be checked against the legislation and KRA guidance. A Kenyan resident freelancer should not assume that SEP replaces their ordinary personal or business income-tax obligations.</p><h2>How is the tax calculated?</h2><p>KRA's January 2025 iTax enhancements explain a deemed taxable profit of 10% of declared turnover and 30% tax on that deemed profit, an effective amount of 3% of the declared turnover under that computation. This is a description of KRA's implemented 2025 system, not a guarantee that the same treatment applies to every 2026 transaction or that all proposed regulations are enacted.</p><h2>How does a business register?</h2><p>KRA's <a href=\"https://kra.go.ke/images/publications/Step-by-Step-Guide-for-Significant-Economic-Tax.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">iTax SEP registration guide</a> explains the tax-obligation process, including the replacement of the DST obligation for eligible taxpayers. Non-resident businesses should get professional advice where permanent establishment, treaty, withholding tax, VAT or excluded services may affect compliance.</p><h2>What changed in the Finance Act 2026?</h2><p>Tax rules for digital services continue to evolve. KRA's <a href=\"https://www.kra.go.ke/popular-links/finance-act-2026-what-it-means-for-you\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">Finance Act 2026 overview</a> describes several effective dates and sector-specific changes. Before filing a 2026 return, check the enacted statute and current KRA implementation notices rather than relying on a draft regulation.</p><h2>SEP tax versus DST</h2><p>DST applied to relevant earlier tax periods; SEP is the successor regime. Historic DST obligations can remain payable for pre-transition periods. Do not file both on the same income simply because both labels appear in older records.</p><p><em>Reviewed 22 October 2026. Compliance rules and online procedures can change. Rely on the official KRA iTax portal for amounts due and statutory filing requirements.</em></p>",
