    metaTitle: "Guide: Hire First Employee in Kenya & Setup KRA NSSF SHIF",
    metaDescription: "Kenyan first employee compliance guide for PAYE, NSSF 2026 limits, SHIF, housing levy, filing deadlines and true employer costs.",
    content: "<h2>Hiring your first employee in Kenya</h2><p>Hiring creates separate employer responsibilities for PAYE, NSSF, SHIF and the Affordable Housing Levy. These are not one combined payroll tax, and the employer's own contributions should not be deducted twice from the employee's salary.</p><h2>Employer setup checklist</h2><ol><li>Confirm employment contracts, staff identity, tax status and pensionable earnings.</li><li>Ensure your organisation's KRA PIN and PAYE obligation are in order.</li><li>Register and maintain employer details with the National Social Security Fund.</li><li>Set up the Social Health Authority employer portal and contribution process.</li><li>Configure Affordable Housing Levy deductions and matching employer payments.</li><li>Use an auditable monthly payroll with payslips, filings and bank or government payment receipts.</li></ol><h2>2026 recurring statutory amounts</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Employee</th><th>Employer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NSSF</td><td>Generally 6% of pensionable earnings, capped at KSh 6,480/month from February 2026</td><td>Matching 6%, with same upper cap</td></tr><tr><td>SHIF</td><td>Generally 2.75% of gross salary, subject to SHA assessment</td><td>Administers deduction and remittance, not a matching 2.75%</td></tr><tr><td>Affordable Housing Levy</td><td>1.5% of gross salary</td><td>Separate matching 1.5%</td></tr><tr><td>PAYE</td><td>Progressive tax depending on taxable pay</td><td>Deducts, files and remits the employee's PAYE</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Employer deadlines</h2><p>Monthly PAYE is generally due by the ninth day of the following month. SHA's employer notice identifies the ninth day each month, while KRA's Affordable Housing Levy notice uses the ninth working day after the relevant month. Check the current NSSF and other remittance obligations directly with each agency.</p><h2>Budgeting for the true cost of employment</h2><p>Beyond agreed gross salary, employers should plan for their own NSSF and AHL share, leave, insurance, equipment, statutory benefits, payroll administration and any contractual commitments. Avoid using a flat made-up employment-cost multiplier for every worker.</p><h2>Further guidance</h2><p>Read our detailed <a href=\"/blog/the-complete-guide-to-nssf-contributions-in-kenya/\">NSSF contributions</a>, <a href=\"/blog/understanding-shif-deductions-in-kenya-and-what-replaced-nhif/\">SHIF</a> and <a href=\"/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paye-tax-in-kenya/\">PAYE</a> guides. Official sources: <a href=\"https://www.kra.go.ke/individual/filing-paying/types-of-taxes/paye\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">KRA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.nssf.or.ke/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">NSSF</a> and <a href=\"https://employers.sha.go.ke/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">SHA</a>.</p><p><em>Reviewed 22 October 2026. Compliance rules and online procedures can change. Rely on the official KRA iTax portal for amounts due and statutory filing requirements.</em></p>",
